Computational Meditation for Language Models

An Adaptive Offline-Reflection and Memory-Consolidation Framework for Uncertainty-Aware Artificial Agents

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

Large language models and autonomous agents increasingly employ additional computation for reasoning, self-reflection, verification, memory retrieval, and post-interaction learning. This paper proposes Computational Meditation (CM), a functional architectural principle in which an artificial agent temporarily suspends immediate action and allocates a bounded computational budget to uncertainty estimation, self-evaluation, contradiction detection, memory replay, verification, and knowledge consolidation before returning to active operation. The term meditation is used strictly in a computational sense and does not imply consciousness, phenomenology, or biological fatigue.

We model CM as an uncertainty-triggered hybrid control policy alternating between Active and Meditative computational regimes. The central theoretical contributions are: (i) an Adaptive Meditation Threshold Theorem establishing that optimal meditation duration is non-decreasing in epistemic uncertainty and that meditation is rational if and only if uncertainty exceeds a computable threshold; (ii) a Memory-Safety Contraction Theorem proving that verified consolidation drives memory-induced decision risk toward a bounded steady state at a geometric rate; and (iii) a Network Memory-Safety Contraction Theorem extending this guarantee to multi-agent systems under a contraction condition on local verifier error. We further establish a correspondence between the CM objective and Expected Free Energy minimization under specific generative-model assumptions, and we provide a detailed operationalization of the value function via Process Reward Models. No experimental results are reported; this is a pre-experimental theoretical contribution with explicitly specified falsification protocols.

---

1. Introduction

Current language-model inference is frequently conceptualized as a stateless function mapping inputs to outputs. Contemporary systems, however, increasingly deviate from this picture: they generate intermediate reasoning traces, critique their own outputs, retrieve and update memories, invoke external tools, verify candidate solutions, revise answers, and consolidate information across interaction boundaries. Recent work on test-time compute scaling (Snell et al., 2024), self-refinement (Madaan et al., 2023), reflective agents (Shinn et al., 2023), sleep-time compute (Anonymous, 2025), and replay-based consolidation suggests a broader architectural principle:

\text{Experience} \rightarrow \text{Reflection} \rightarrow \text{Verification} \rightarrow \text{Consolidation} \rightarrow \text{Future Action}

The central question addressed by this paper is whether these mechanisms can be unified into an adaptive control regime that decides when an agent should suspend immediate action and allocate computational resources to internal reflection and consolidation. We propose such a regime, termed Computational Meditation (CM), and develop a formal framework for its analysis and implementation.

This paper makes four principal contributions. First, we define a state-dependent trigger function driven by epistemic uncertainty, recent error rates, contradiction levels, memory interference, and estimated decision risk. Second, we formulate the meditation budget allocation problem as a continuous optimization over expected decision quality per unit of computation. Third, we prove structural properties of the optimal policy, including monotonicity in uncertainty and a threshold characterization of meditation rationality. Fourth, we establish geometric contraction bounds on memory-induced decision risk for both single-agent and multi-agent settings, providing safety guarantees for verified memory consolidation.

The framework is intentionally falsifiable. We provide explicit experimental protocols in Section 7 and define the conditions under which the theoretical predictions would be refuted. No experimental results are reported; the contribution is a mathematically precise and operationally grounded theoretical foundation for adaptive offline cognition in artificial agents.

