2. Terminological Clarification

The term meditation is functional and computational. We make no claim that language models experience consciousness, fatigue, introspection, or subjective states. Computational Meditation is distinct from biological or contemplative meditation in all respects.

Computational meditation is defined as an engineered computational process satisfying five conditions:

1. Temporary suspension or reduction of immediate action in the environment;
2. Additional internal computation beyond what is required for the next action;
3. Evaluation of the agent's current epistemic state, including uncertainty and error signals;
4. Possible modification or consolidation of persistent representations in external memory;
5. Return to active operation after a bounded computational interval.

---

3. Relationship to Existing Research

This work intersects several active research threads. We position CM relative to each, emphasizing the distinctive contribution.

Self-refinement and Reflexion. Self-refinement methods (Madaan et al., 2023) and Reflexion-style agents (Shinn et al., 2023) employ reflection operators that critique and revise outputs. These frameworks provide the reflection mechanism but lack a principled trigger function for deciding when reflection is worthwhile and how much computation to allocate. CM generalizes these approaches by making the reflection decision adaptive and budget-optimal.

Test-time compute scaling. Snell et al. (2024) derive scaling laws for test-time compute allocation, showing that optimal compute per prompt increases with task difficulty. Their framework allocates compute within a single response; CM extends this to the offline, stateful setting where computation affects persistent memory and future performance, not just the current output.

Quiet-STaR and DeepSeek-R1. Quiet-STaR (Zelikman et al., 2022) and DeepSeek-R1 (Guo et al., 2025) demonstrate that internal reasoning and self-verification can emerge from training. DeepSeek-R1-Zero showed that reflection behaviors arise spontaneously from reinforcement learning without hand-engineered meditation modules (Guo et al., 2025). CM is complementary: it provides an architectural framework for controlling such behaviors, not generating them.

Sleep-time compute. Recent work (Anonymous, 2025; Anonymous, 2025) explores offline computation for memory consolidation, reporting substantial reductions in test-time compute requirements. Sleep-time compute is primarily a latency optimization—pre-computing results that would otherwise be computed at test time. CM differs in three respects: (i) it is triggered by state-dependent uncertainty rather than a fixed schedule; (ii) it optimizes the allocation of offline compute based on expected decision quality improvement; (iii) it provides provable safety guarantees on memory-induced risk.

Agentic uncertainty quantification. AUQ (Anonymous, 2025) uses epistemic uncertainty to trigger a System 1/System 2 distinction, where high uncertainty invokes deeper reasoning. CM shares the uncertainty-triggered architecture but adds offline consolidation, memory safety, and a formal budget optimization theory.

Process reward models. The PRM literature (Lightman et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2023; Anonymous, 2024; Anonymous, 2025) provides trainable components for scoring intermediate reasoning steps. Section 5 establishes the connection between CM's verification operator and PRMs, grounding the framework in an implementable component.

Active inference. Active Inference (Friston et al., 2017) provides a variational principle for action and perception. Section 6 establishes a correspondence between the CM objective and Expected Free Energy minimization under specific generative-model assumptions, clarifying the scope of this connection.

Auton framework. The Auton architecture (Anonymous, 2025) offers declarative agent construction but lacks an explicit trigger function and memory-safety guarantees. CM provides these missing components.

The distinctive contribution of CM is the joint treatment of adaptive triggering, optimal offline budget allocation, and provable memory-safety contraction. To our knowledge, no existing framework combines all three.

