4. Formal Framework

4.1 Agent State

An artificial agent at time  t  is represented by the tuple

X_t = (K_t, M_t, U_t, G_t, R_t, C_t),

where:

·  K_t  denotes accessible knowledge (facts, rules, procedures);
·  M_t = (M_t^E, M_t^S)  comprises episodic memory  M_t^E  and semantic memory  M_t^S ;
·  U_t  denotes epistemic uncertainty, a scalar measure of the agent's doubt about its current state;
·  G_t  denotes current goals and task specification;
·  R_t  denotes the reasoning state, including intermediate computations and working memory;
·  C_t  denotes computational resources available.

The agent operates under two regimes:

q_t \in \{A, M\},

where  A  denotes Active and  M  denotes Meditative.

4.2 Active Computation

During active operation ( q_t = A ), the agent selects an action  a_t  via policy  \pi_A , receives an observation  o_{t+1} , and stores the experience

e_t = (X_t, a_t, o_{t+1}, r_t)

in episodic memory  M_t^E .

4.3 Meditation Trigger Function

The trigger score is defined as

S(X_t) = \alpha U_t + \beta E_t + \gamma \Gamma_t + \delta I_t + \epsilon R_t,

where  U_t  is epistemic uncertainty,  E_t  is recent error rate,  \Gamma_t  is contradiction level,  I_t  is memory interference, and  R_t  is estimated decision risk, with nonnegative weights  \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta, \epsilon .

Epistemic uncertainty  U_t  is computed via semantic entropy over stochastic sampling rollouts:

U_t = -\sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}_t} p(c \mid X_t) \log p(c \mid X_t),

where  \mathcal{C}_t  is the set of semantic equivalence classes among sampled rollouts.

Contradiction score  \Gamma_t  is the average pairwise semantic divergence between candidate trajectories:

\Gamma_t = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{Y}_t|(|\mathcal{Y}_t| - 1)} \sum_{y_i \neq y_j \in \mathcal{Y}_t} d_{\text{sem}}(y_i, y_j),

where  \mathcal{Y}_t  is the set of candidate outputs and  d_{\text{sem}}  is a semantic distance metric.

Memory interference  I_t  is the softmax overlap between new episodic memory keys and existing semantic memory keys:

I_t = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{K}_{\text{new}}|} \sum_{k \in \mathcal{K}_{\text{new}}} \max_{k' \in \mathcal{K}_{\text{sem}}} \text{sim}(k, k'),

where  \mathcal{K}_{\text{new}}  and  \mathcal{K}_{\text{sem}}  are key sets for new and semantic memories, respectively.

Recent error rate  E_t  is the empirical error rate over the last  N_{\text{win}}  interactions:

E_t = \frac{1}{N_{\text{win}}} \sum_{i=t-N_{\text{win}}+1}^{t} \mathbb{1}[r_i < \tau_{\text{err}}].

Estimated decision risk  R_t  is the expected cost of the current decision under uncertainty:

R_t = \mathbb{E}_{a \sim \pi_A(\cdot \mid X_t)} \left[ \mathbb{E}_{o \sim p(\cdot \mid X_t, a)} [c(o)] \right],

where  c(o)  is a cost function.

The agent enters meditation when

S(X_t) > \theta_t,

where  \theta_t  is an adaptive threshold updated based on meditation efficacy:

\theta_{t+1} = \theta_t + \eta_\theta^+ \cdot \mathbb{1}[\Delta J_t < 0] - \eta_\theta^- \cdot \mathbb{1}[\Delta J_t > 0],

with  \eta_\theta^+ > \eta_\theta^- > 0  reflecting asymmetric adjustment costs.

4.4 Meditative Transformation Operator

During meditation, the agent applies

\mathcal{M}_T = \mathcal{I} \circ \mathcal{V} \circ \mathcal{C} \circ \mathcal{R} \circ \mathcal{P},

where:

·  \mathcal{P} : Memory replay—samples high-uncertainty episodes from  M_t^E ;
·  \mathcal{R} : Reflection—generates verbal lessons and critiques;
·  \mathcal{C} : Consolidation—summarizes into candidate persistent memories;
·  \mathcal{V} : Verification—checks candidates against confidence threshold  \tau_{\text{accept}} ;
·  \mathcal{I} : Insertion—adds verified candidates to semantic memory  M_t^S .

The parameter  T  is the computational meditation budget. Verification precedes insertion by construction, ensuring that only verified candidates enter persistent memory.

