5. Operationalizing the Value Function via Process Reward Models

A central challenge in the CM framework is the definition and estimation of the value function  V(X) , which measures expected future decision quality. We propose operationalizing  V(X)  via Process Reward Models (PRMs), which score intermediate reasoning steps and have become a standard component in test-time compute systems (Lightman et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2023; Anonymous, 2024).

5.1 PRM-Based Value Estimation

Let  \text{PRM}(X, s)  denote the score assigned by a process reward model to a reasoning step  s  in state  X . We define the value function as

V(X) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi_A(\cdot \mid X)} \left[ \text{PRM}(X, s) \right],

where the expectation is taken over the distribution of next reasoning steps induced by the agent's policy. This operationalization has three advantages: (i) PRMs are trainable from process-level supervision; (ii) PRM scores are bounded and interpretable; (iii) the expectation can be estimated via Monte Carlo sampling.

5.2 Mapping CM Operators to PRM Concepts

The meditative transformation operator  \mathcal{M}_T  maps onto PRM concepts as follows:

· The verification operator  \mathcal{V}  is a PRM (or ensemble of PRMs) scoring candidate memories. A candidate memory  m  is accepted if  \text{PRM}(X, m) > \tau_{\text{accept}} .
· The reflection operator  \mathcal{R}  corresponds to the critique generation step in generative PRMs such as GenPRM (Anonymous, 2025).
· The Memory Safety Margin  \Delta_{\text{safe}}  (defined in Section 8.2) is a risk-adjusted acceptance threshold analogous to the confidence thresholds used in adaptive PRMs (Anonymous, 2025).

5.3 Verifier Independence and Bias

A critical issue in PRM-based verification is the correlation between generator and verifier errors. If the verifier is the same model as the generator, systematic biases in the generator will be shared by the verifier, undermining the independence assumption required for the sub-Gaussian sample complexity analysis (Section 8.1). We define the following requirement:

Verifier Independence Condition. The verifier  \mathcal{V}  must be sufficiently independent of the generator  \pi_A  such that the correlation coefficient  \rho  between generator errors and verifier errors satisfies

\rho < \rho_{\max} < 1,

where  \rho_{\max}  is a threshold determined by the safety analysis.

In practice, this condition can be satisfied by: (i) using a different model family as verifier (e.g., a specialized smaller model); (ii) incorporating symbolic verification tools such as theorem provers (e.g., Z3, Isabelle) for formal domains; or (iii) using ensemble disagreement across multiple verifiers. The confabulation analysis in Section 8.3 provides additional motivation for verifier independence.

