6. Meditation as an Optimization Problem

Let  V(X)  denote the expected future decision quality of state  X , operationalized via PRM as in Section 5. Let  C(T) = cT  denote the computational cost of meditation duration  T , where  c > 0  is the unit cost per computation step.

6.1 Meditation Gain

The meditation gain is

G_M(X, T) = V(\mathcal{M}_T(X)) - V(X),

representing the improvement in expected decision quality resulting from meditation of duration  T .

6.2 Meditation Return on Compute

The meditation return on compute is

\text{MRC}(X, T) = \frac{G_M(X, T)}{C(T)}.

6.3 Net Value and Optimal Budget

The net value of meditation is

J(T \mid X) = G_M(X, T) - \lambda C(T),

where  \lambda \geq 0  is a cost weight parameter. The optimal meditation budget is

T^*(X) = \arg\max_{T \in [0, T_{\max}]} J(T \mid X).

This formulation converts the decision of how much to meditate into a continuous optimization problem. The central mathematical object of the CM framework is  T^*(X) , the optimal allocation of computational resources to internal reflection and consolidation.

6.4 Meta-Reasoning Cost

Computing the trigger score  S(X_t)  incurs computational cost. Let  C_{\text{trigger}}  denote this cost. The true net value of meditation, accounting for meta-reasoning, is

J_{\text{true}}(T \mid X) = G_M(X, T) - \lambda \left( C(T) + C_{\text{trigger}} \cdot \mathbb{1}[T > 0] \right).

This formulation reveals a break-even condition: meditation is rational only if the expected gain exceeds both the meditation cost and the trigger evaluation cost. For large meditation budgets and cheap trigger heuristics,  C_{\text{trigger}}  is negligible; for small budgets or expensive triggers (e.g., full semantic entropy computation), the meta-reasoning cost may dominate.

