10. Engineering Implementation

10.1 Algorithmic Skeleton

We provide a minimal algorithmic skeleton for implementing a CM agent. Pseudocode is provided in Algorithm 1.

Algorithm 1: Computational Meditation Agent

```
Initialize: X_0, θ_0, M_0^S = ∅
for t = 0, 1, 2, ...:
    # Active regime
    if q_t == A:
        Compute trigger score S(X_t)
        if S(X_t) > θ_t:
            q_t = M
            T* = argmax_{T ∈ [0, T_max]} [V̂(M_T(X_t)) - V̂(X_t) - λcT]
            Run M_{T*}(X_t)
            Update X_{t+1} = M_{T*}(X_t)
            Update θ_{t+1} based on ΔJ_t
            q_{t+1} = A
        else:
            Select action a_t via π_A
            Observe o_{t+1}
            Store e_t = (X_t, a_t, o_{t+1}, r_t) in M_t^E
            X_{t+1} = F_A(X_t, a_t, o_{t+1})
```

10.2 Trigger Computation Cost

The trigger score  S(X_t)  requires computing semantic entropy, contradiction scores, memory interference, and error rates. We recommend a hierarchical approach:

1. Cheap trigger: Monitor only recent error rate  E_t  and memory interference  I_t , which can be computed from cached statistics.
2. Full trigger: If the cheap trigger exceeds a lower threshold  \theta_{\text{low}} , compute the full trigger score  S(X_t)  including semantic entropy and decision risk.

The cost model for the trigger is:

C_{\text{trigger}} = C_{\text{cheap}} + p_{\text{full}} \cdot C_{\text{full}},

where  p_{\text{full}}  is the probability of crossing the lower threshold. The break-even condition for meditation is:

G_M(X, T) > \lambda (C(T) + C_{\text{trigger}}).

