12. Failure Modes and Limitations

12.1 Over-Meditation

The agent may allocate excessive compute to reflection, reducing active performance. This occurs when  \lambda  is set too low or when the trigger threshold  \theta_t  is set too low. The net value formulation  J(T) = G_M(T) - \lambda C(T)  provides a principled way to avoid over-meditation, but only if  V(X)  and  C(T)  are accurately estimated.

12.2 Verification Failure

If the verifier  \Phi_V  fails to detect hallucinations—particularly confabulated memories that are confident and plausible—the memory-safety guarantee fails. The contraction condition  \gamma < 1  is critical; if  \gamma \geq 1  for specific error classes, the framework cannot guarantee safety. This motivates the Verifier Independence Condition (Section 5.3) and the confabulation analysis (Section 8.3).

12.3 Consolidation Collapse

Over-aggressive deduplication or generalization may merge distinct correct memories into an incorrect schema, increasing risk rather than decreasing it. This violates Assumption A8 (deduplication non-expansiveness). The safety gate  \hat{\mathcal{R}}(M^S \cup \{m\}) \leq R_{\max}  provides a mechanism to detect and prevent this.

12.4 Trigger Miscalibration

The trigger threshold  \theta_t  may be set too high (causing missed meditations) or too low (causing excessive meditation). The adaptive threshold update rule (Section 4.3) is designed to correct this over time, but it requires reliable estimates of  \Delta J_t , which may be noisy.

12.5 Value Function Estimation Bias

The value function  V(X)  must be estimated from samples. If the estimator is biased—e.g., due to correlated verifier errors—the  2\varepsilon  bound in Lemma 1 does not capture the full error. The bias must be analyzed separately (Section 5.3).

---

13. Conclusion

We have presented a mathematically precise framework for Computational Meditation as an adaptive hybrid-control regime for allocating offline cognition in language agents. The central contributions are:

1. A state-dependent trigger function driven by epistemic uncertainty, error rates, contradiction levels, memory interference, and decision risk, with an adaptive threshold.
2. An optimization formulation that converts the meditation decision into a continuous budget allocation problem, maximizing expected decision quality per unit of computation.
3. Theoretical guarantees including the Adaptive Meditation Threshold Theorem (Theorem 2), the Memory-Safety Contraction Theorem (Theorem 3), and the Network Memory-Safety Contraction Theorem (Theorem 4).
4. An operationalization of the value function via Process Reward Models, connecting the framework to a trainable, implementable component.
5. Explicit falsification protocols that specify how each theoretical prediction can be empirically tested and refuted.

The framework is intentionally falsifiable and does not rely on any claim of subjective experience. We invite empirical testing and further theoretical refinement.

The resulting agent does not need to be conscious, tired, or biologically alive. It simply needs to know when acting immediately is inferior to spending computation on becoming better prepared to act later.

