Appendix D: Detailed Proof of Proposition 1 (Sub-Gaussian Sample Complexity)

D.1 Statement

Suppose  \widehat{V}_T  is the average of  N  independent sub-Gaussian samples with variance proxy  \sigma^2 , i.e.,

\widehat{V}_T = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N v_i, \quad \mathbb{E}[v_i] = V(\mathcal{M}_T(X)), \quad \mathbb{P}(|v_i - V| \ge t) \le 2\exp\left(-\frac{t^2}{2\sigma^2}\right).

For a discrete budget set  \mathcal{T} = \{1, 2, \ldots, T_{\max}\} , the uniform bound

\sup_{T \in \mathcal{T}} |\widehat{V}_T - V_T| \le \varepsilon

holds with probability at least  1-\delta  provided

N \ge \frac{2\sigma^2}{\varepsilon^2} \ln\left( \frac{2T_{\max}}{\delta} \right).

D.2 Proof

For fixed  T , Hoeffding's inequality for sub-Gaussian random variables gives:

\mathbb{P}\left( |\widehat{V}_T - V(\mathcal{M}_T(X))| \ge \varepsilon \right) \le 2\exp\left(-\frac{N \varepsilon^2}{2\sigma^2}\right).

A union bound over  \mathcal{T}  yields:

\mathbb{P}\left( \sup_{T \in \mathcal{T}} |\widehat{V}_T - V_T| \ge \varepsilon \right) \le \sum_{T \in \mathcal{T}} \mathbb{P}(|\widehat{V}_T - V_T| \ge \varepsilon) \le 2T_{\max}\exp\left(-\frac{N\varepsilon^2}{2\sigma^2}\right).

Setting the right-hand side equal to  \delta  and solving for  N :

2T_{\max}\exp\left(-\frac{N\varepsilon^2}{2\sigma^2}\right) = \delta \iff \exp\left(-\frac{N\varepsilon^2}{2\sigma^2}\right) = \frac{\delta}{2T_{\max}} \iff \frac{N\varepsilon^2}{2\sigma^2} = \ln\left(\frac{2T_{\max}}{\delta}\right).

Therefore:

N \ge \frac{2\sigma^2}{\varepsilon^2} \ln\left( \frac{2T_{\max}}{\delta} \right).

∎

D.3 Remarks on Continuous Budget Sets

For continuous  T \in [0, T_{\max}] , a covering number argument can be used. Suppose the budget interval is covered by  m  points with spacing  \Delta T = T_{\max} / m . If the value function is  L -Lipschitz in  T , then uniform error  \varepsilon/2  on the covering points implies uniform error  \varepsilon  on the entire interval, provided  L \cdot \Delta T \le \varepsilon/2 . The required number of covering points is  m = \lceil 2LT_{\max}/\varepsilon \rceil , yielding sample complexity:

N \ge \frac{2\sigma^2}{(\varepsilon/2)^2} \ln\left( \frac{2m}{\delta} \right) = \frac{8\sigma^2}{\varepsilon^2} \ln\left( \frac{4LT_{\max}}{\varepsilon\delta} \right).

This is only marginally worse than the discrete case.

D.4 Important Caveat

The sub-Gaussian assumption requires that verifier samples are independent and identically distributed. In practice, multiple calls to the same LLM verifier on the same input are not independent: they are deterministic functions of the same underlying model. The assumption may be approximately satisfied if the verifier uses stochastic sampling (e.g., different random seeds, different prompt phrasings), but the independence condition should be scrutinized empirically. Systematic verifier biases (e.g., consistently failing to detect certain types of hallucinations) contribute to estimator bias, which is not reduced by increasing  N . The sample complexity result bounds variance, not bias.

