Appendix J: Supplementary Technical Remarks

J.1 Boundary Cases in Theorem 2

If the optimal budget is at the upper boundary  T_{\max} , the first-order condition  J'(T^*) = 0  need not hold with equality. In that case,  T^* = T_{\max}  and monotonicity saturates: further increases in  U  do not increase  T^* . To guarantee strict monotonicity for all  U , one would need  h'(T_{\max}) = 0  or  T_{\max} = \infty . This is an important caveat for finite-budget implementations.

J.2 Non-Linear Cost

If  C(T)  is convex increasing with  C'(T) > 0 , the threshold condition becomes:

g(U) h'(0) > C'(0),

and the threshold is:

\theta^* = g^{-1}\left( \frac{C'(0)}{h'(0)} \right).

The monotonicity result holds as long as  C''(T) \ge 0  and  h''(T) < 0 , ensuring strict concavity of  J .

J.3 Verification Ordering and Safety

The operator  \mathcal{M}_T = \mathcal{I} \circ \mathcal{V} \circ \mathcal{C} \circ \mathcal{R} \circ \mathcal{P}  ensures that verification precedes insertion. In Theorem 3, this ordering is crucial: if insertion occurred before verification, the risk reduction guarantee would fail. The safety constraint  \mathcal{R}(M_{t+1}) \le \mathcal{R}(M_t) + \epsilon_M  is only meaningful when  \mathcal{V}  filters candidates before they enter  M^S .

J.4 Hybrid System Formulation

The full system can be written as a hybrid dynamical system:

q_{t+1} = \begin{cases} M, & S(X_t) > \theta_t, \\ A, & S(X_t) \le \theta_t, \end{cases}

X_{t+1} = \begin{cases} F_A(X_t, a_t, \omega_t), & q_t = A, \\ F_M(X_t, T_t, \xi_t), & q_t = M, \end{cases}

where  F_A  is the active transition function,  F_M  the meditative transition function,  \omega_t  environmental stochasticity, and  \xi_t  internal stochasticity. This formulation makes explicit that CM is a state-triggered control policy, not a fixed schedule.

J.5 Estimator Details

In practice, the value function  V(X)  may be estimated via:

1. Self-consistency: sample multiple rollouts, compute average reward or correctness;
2. Separate critic model: train a lightweight value head on agent trajectories;
3. Calibration-based proxy: use confidence scores as a surrogate for value.

The sub-Gaussian assumption on the estimator is reasonable when the estimator is an average of bounded or sub-Gaussian rewards. However, verifier independence must be assessed empirically.

J.6 Computational Cost of Trigger Computation

The trigger function  S(X_t)  requires computing semantic entropy, contradiction scores, and memory interference. This incurs non-trivial computational cost. A full analysis of CM must account for the meta-reasoning cost of deciding whether to meditate. We recommend a hierarchical trigger: a cheap approximation monitors only recent error rate and memory interference; full trigger computation occurs only when the cheap approximation crosses a lower threshold.

J.7 Confabulation and Verifier Independence

Recent work on memory confabulation in reflexive agents (Anonymous, 2026) shows that reflective memory can systematically store incorrect but confident reflections. In the CM framework, this corresponds to a verifier failure mode where  \gamma \ge 1  for specific error classes. To mitigate this, we propose:

· Programmatic failure-signal extraction: replace open-ended self-diagnosis with deterministic checks.
· Hybrid verification: combine neural PRM scoring with symbolic tools (e.g., theorem provers, database lookups).
· Safety margin monitoring: if  \Delta_{\text{safe}} < 0 , trigger a response to increase verifier independence or reduce acceptance threshold.

